Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:56:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?[daveey]=20lifecycle=20guard:=20walk=20every=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?line=20bash=20may=20end=20a=20$(=E2=80=A6)=20heredoc=20delimite?=
 =?UTF-8?q?r=20at?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Review 5905862108 on PR #3020 (request changes): bash ends a heredoc whose
delimiter holds $(…) at its own reprint of the parsed command, not at the
literal text. `<<$(echo  E|cat)` ends at `$(echo E | cat)`, `<<$([[ a ]])`
at `$([[ -n a ]])`, and `<<"$(echo  E)"` (double quotes too) at `$(echo E)`.
Reading the word verbatim predicted the wrong end line, so the body's `}`
still closed an outer group early and `cat f.py` escaped the `} | python3`.

Fix (fail closed rather than model bash's printer):
- tools/shell_heredoc_words.py: reprinted_by_bash() flags a word holding
  $( (or a $(( that is not plain arithmetic). _balanced_end skips a nested
  $(…)/${…} whole, so ${x:-$(echo })} reads as one word.
- tools/shell_heredoc.py: _parse_heredoc_operator returns None for such a
  word (also inside double quotes), so no masker or span walk trusts a guessed
  terminator. heredoc_body_walk() also reports where the span walk stopped.
- cron/lifecycle_guard_groups.py: heredoc_scripts() additionally yields one
  view per later line holding $( (bash's reprint always does), with the
  opener's heredoc words and the body through that line removed. Views are
  capped at 16 per command; heredoc_views_overflow() reports a cut-off.
- cron/lifecycle_guard.py (+8 lines, _walk_refusal): past the cap, executed text fails
  closed with a named refusal telling the model to use a plain word.

Backticks, $((…)), ${…} without $(, $'…' and $"…" keep exact parsing;
each terminator in the test file is checked against real bash 5.2.

Tests: tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_heredoc_delimiters.py (96 cases).
